@node Scaling Up
@section Scale, parallelism, and interactivity

Differential dataflow is meant to scale in three directions: the amount
of data, the number of workers, and the rate of interaction.  Each is a
small change to the skip-level program of the previous section.

@subsection Increase the scale

Ten people is a small organization; the same program handles ten
million.  Nothing in the dataflow changes, only the input loop bound.
Because the result is maintained incrementally, applying ten million
single-person changes after the initial load costs far less than ten
million recomputations; in practice it is well under a microsecond per
change.  (Turn off the per-record @code{inspect} first, or printing
dominates the time.)

@subsection Increase the parallelism

Differential dataflow runs across many workers and produces the same
output.  The one thing to get right is that each worker should load only
its share of the input, not the whole thing.  A worker knows its own
index and the total count:

@example
for (person = dtfl_worker_index(w); person < size;
     person += dtfl_worker_peers(w)) @{
        unsigned char buf[16];
        dtfl_put_u64(buf, person / 2);
        dtfl_put_u64(buf + 8, person);
        dtfl_input_session_insert(input, buf, sizeof buf);
@}
@end example

Each worker inserts the people whose number is its index modulo the peer
count, and the same partition applies to the changes.  Select the worker
count and communication mode through @code{dtfl_config}
(@pxref{Installation}); the dataflow is identical.

@subsection Increase the interactivity

Rather than load every change and wait for the final result, a program
can apply one change and await its output before supplying the next.
The tool is the same probe loop as in timely (@pxref{Running Timely
Dataflows}): attach a probe to the end of the dataflow, and after each
change advance the input, flush it, and step until the probe catches up.

@example
dtfl_input_session_advance_to(input, &next);
dtfl_input_session_flush(input);
while (dtfl_probe_less_than(probe, dtfl_input_session_time(input)))
        dtfl_worker_step(w);
@end example

The flush is what pushes the buffered changes into the dataflow; the
step loop runs the computation until the output reflects the input's
current time.  This turns the batch program into an interactive one, one
change in and its consequences out, at the latency of a single update.
